.SH "SQL QUERIES"
.B duscope query
.I PATH
.B \-\-sql
.I STATEMENT
runs one read\-only SQL statement over the tables of a saved scan: the
newest that holds
.I PATH
(the current directory by default), or the one
.B \-\-scan
names.
When none holds it,
.B query
scans
.I PATH
and saves the scan first, as the list verbs do;
.B \-\-no\-save
keeps that scan's index in a temporary directory, removed afterwards, and
.B \-\-no\-scan
fails instead.
.B duscope schema
lists the tables and their columns, and
.I docs/index\-format.md
in the source distribution describes them: a row for each directory in
.BR dirs ,
one for each other entry in
.BR files ,
names as BLOBs, sizes in bytes and times in nanoseconds.
.SS "What a statement may do"
One statement, a
.BR SELECT ,
which reads the index's tables
.RB ( scan_meta ,
.BR scan_volumes ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR files ,
.BR hardlinks ,
.BR clones ,
.BR shared_members ,
.B scan_errors
and
.BR type_stats ),
through subqueries and common table expressions, recursive ones included,
and calls SQLite's core, date and time and JSON functions and duscope's
own; an aggregate may run over a window
.RB ( "sum(allocated) OVER ()" ).
The saved scan is opened read\-only, and the statement is held to that:
a write, a
.BR PRAGMA ,
.BR ATTACH ,
a transaction, a second statement, a table that is not the index's (the
schema, a view, a table\-valued function such as
.BR json_each ),
a window\-only function such as
.BR row_number ,
and any other function are refused before a row is read, with
.B sql_denied
(exit 1).
A statement SQLite cannot parse is a usage error (exit 2).
.SS "Functions and parameters"
.TP
.BI path( id )
The entry's absolute path: text, or a BLOB where it is not UTF\-8.
.TP
.BI human_size( bytes )
A size as the tables show it, such as
.BR "412.3 GiB" .
.TP
.BI under( id ", " root_id )
1 when
.I id
is
.I root_id
or an entry below it, else 0.
.TP
.BR :root_id ", " :root_last
The entry of
.I PATH
and the last id below it, so that its subtree is
.BR "id BETWEEN :root_id AND :root_last" .
.SS Limits
A statement without a
.B LIMIT
of its own returns 25 rows at most (or
.BR \-\-max\-rows ,
when that is lower); one with a
.B LIMIT
returns
.B \-\-max\-rows
at most, 10,000 by default and at most.
A row past the limit sets
.B truncated
in the result, and the summary line says how to get more.
.B \-\-timeout
stops the statement after 5 seconds, or the seconds it gives, 60 at most,
with
.B sql_timeout
(exit 1), which a narrower statement or a longer timeout may avoid.
The statement may use 256 MiB of memory, its sorts included, its rows may
hold 256 MiB, and a value may be 1,000,000 bytes long; past any of them it
is refused with
.BR sql_denied .
.SS Output
Under
.B \-\-json
the result holds
.BR columns ,
.BR rows ,
.B row_count
and
.BR truncated ,
in the statement's order, a BLOB as
.BR {\(dq$bytes\(dq:\ \(dq <base64> \(dq} .
.B \-\-plain
writes a line of the column names, then a tab\-separated record for each
row, a BLOB as its bytes and NULL as nothing.
A table is for people.
